complexity.why:
Let M = 10^6 (hard-coded upper bound), N = len(var_2). The dominant loop is:
for i in range(2, M):
    s = 0
    for c in var_7[i::i]:
        s += c

The slice var_7[i::i] has length ≈ M/i. Total work is Σ_{i=2..M} M/i ≈ M log M operations, and each slice creation is also O(M/i), so total time is Θ(M log M). The gcd loop adds O(N log A_max), but this is negligible compared to O(M log M). Space is dominated by var_7 of size M+1.

The critical section is:
for var_3 in range(1, n+1):
  for var_4 in range(var_3, n+1):
    var_13 = 0
    for var_5 in range(var_4 - var_3 + 1):
      var_13 += var_2[var_3 + var_5][var_4]
This performs Θ(∑_{L=1}^n ∑_{R=L}^n (R-L+1)) ≈ Θ(n^3/6) iterations in pure Python. Other loops (grid init, filling, row prefix sums) are only O(n^2). Memory holds three (n+1)×(n+1) integer grids plus the query list, hence Θ(n^2) space.
